result:
The bug is a units mismatch. JWT's `exp` field is in **seconds** since epoch, but `Date.now()` returns **milliseconds**. Your comparison is treating them as the same unit, so every token looks like it expires ~1000x further in the future than it actually does.

**Broken:**
```js
if (Date.now() > payload.exp) {  // Date.now() is ~1.7 trillion, exp is ~1.7 billion
  throw new Error('Token expired');
}
// This condition is never true for any reasonable exp value
```

**Fixed — two equivalent options:**
```js
// Option A: convert Date.now() to seconds
if (Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000) > payload.exp) {
  throw new Error('Token expired');
}

// Option B: convert exp to milliseconds
if (Date.now() > payload.exp * 1000) {
  throw new Error('Token expired');
}
```

Option A is idiomatic — it matches how JWT libraries themselves do the comparison.

